            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Near-Surface Air Temperature at 2 meters (T2M)",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Data were obtained from the NASA POWER (Prediction of Worldwide Energy Resources) reanalysis platform (https://power.larc.nasa.gov/). The dataset provides globally consistent, satellite-derived and model-assimilated meteorological variables optimized for solar energy and agricultural applications. For this analysis, two key parameters were downloaded for the period January 2021 to December 2024: 1. All-sky surface shortwave downward irradiance (kWh/m\u00b2/day) 2. Near-Surface Air Temperature at 2 meters (T2M) (\u00b0C)",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Inter-tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The region is a climatically diverse region characterized by complex interactions between the Inter-tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ), monsoon systems, topography, and regional circulation patterns. In East Africa, irradiance varies spatially and temporally due to latitude, altitude, cloud cover, and seasonal weather patterns (Manirambona et al. 2022) The region's climate is strongly modulated by the seasonal migration of the Inter tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ), producing two major rainy seasons and significant cloud- radiation interactions (Camberlin and Philippon 2002; Nicholson 2017).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Inter-tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The observed July irradiance minimum across all countries corresponds to enhanced cloud cover associated with the seasonal northward displacement of the ITCZ, which increases atmospheric convection and reduces incoming shortwave radiation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Inter-tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Generally, the data suggests a strong inverse correlation between rainfall/cloud cover and irradiance, and a direct correlation between irradiance and temperature. Periods of high solar irradiance often precede or coincide with peak temperatures, while periods of low irradiance (due to cloud cover) correlate with cooler months. Both temperature and irradiance peak around March, indicating that maximum solar input drives peak warmth. Both variables hit their lowest point around July when cloud cover is highest. These findings highlight how seasonal cloud patterns, likely influenced by the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone, are the primary drivers for the variability in both temperature and the availability of solar resources in East Africa.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Inter-tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This is largely influenced by the migration of the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Kiremt rainy season",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Ethiopia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "The lowest irradiance values occur during July, with minima around 4.5\u20135.0 kWh/m\u00b2/day, corresponding to the peak of the Kiremt rainy season, when extensive cloud cover substantially suppresses incoming shortwave radiation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "long rains",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Kenya",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "This period coincides with the aftermath of the long rains (March May) and the mid-year cloudy season, which significantly diminish solar radiation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "short rainy season",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Sudan",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "This reduction is linked to Sudan's short rainy season, during which moisture influx and cloud cover increase.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Solar Cycle 25",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The analyzed time period (2021-2024) coincides with the rising phase of Solar Cycle 25, during which solar activity gradually increases. Variations in solar activity produce small fluctuations in total solar irradiance, typically on the order of approximately 0.1% across the solar cycle. Although these variations are relatively small compared to regional cloud-driven variability, solar cycle changes may still contribute to subtle variations in surface radiation and temperature patterns.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Solar Cycle 25",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The study period (2021-2024) coincides with the rising phase of Solar Cycle 25, during which solar activity and solar irradiance gradually increase. Although variations in total solar irradiance are relatively small, they can still contribute to changes in surface radiation and atmospheric temperature. Therefore, part of the observed variations in shortwave downward irradiance and surface temperature over East Africa may also be influenced by solar cycle variability (figure 5).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
